"Graph Labelings: A Prime Area to Explore (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/365716927_Graph_Labelings_A_Prime_Area_to_Explore)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; In particular, classes of graphs shown to be neighborhood-prime include all generalized Petersen graphs, grid graphs of any size, and lobsters given restrictions on the degree of the vertices.\nNeighborhood-prime labeling is a variation of prime labeling. A labeling $f:V(G) \\to [|V(G)|]$ is a neighborhood-prime labeling if for each vertex $v\\in V(G)$ with degree greater than 1, the greatest common divisor of the set of labels in the neighborhood of v is 1. In this paper, we introduce techniques for finding neighborhood-prime labelings based on the Hamiltonicity of the graph, by using conditions on possible degrees of vertices, and by examining a neighborhood graph. In particular, classes of graphs shown to be neighborhood-prime include all generalized Petersen graphs, grid graphs of any size, and lobsters given restrictions on the degree of the vertices. In addition, we show that almost all graphs and almost all regular graphs have are neighborhood-prime, and we find all graphs of order 10 or less that have a neighborhood-prime labeling. We give several conjectures for future work in this area.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nNeighborhood-Prime Labelings of Trees and Other Classes of Graphs\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2018\n\n  * Malori Cloys\n  * Norman Bradley Fox\n\nA neighborhood-prime labeling of a graph is a variation of a prime labeling in which the vertices are assigned labels from 1 to $|V(G)|$ such that the $\\gcd$ of the labels in the neighborhood of each non-degree 1 vertex is equal to 1. In this paper, we examine neighborhood-prime labelings of several classes of graphs such as polygonal snakes and books, with a focus on trees including caterpillars, spiders, and firecrackers.\n\nPrime labelings of generalized Petersen graphs\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2017\n\n  * Steven Schluchter\n  * Justin Zane Schroeder\n  * Kathryn Cokus\n  * Thomas Wilson\n\nView\n\nCoprime and prime labelings of graphs\n\nArticle\n\n  * Apr 2016\n\n  * Adam Berliner\n  * Nathaniel Dean\n  * Jonelle Hook\n  * Cayla D Mcbee\n\nA coprime labeling of a simple graph of order n is a labeling in which adjacent vertices are given relatively prime labels, and a graph is prime if the labels used can be taken to be the first n positive integers. In this paper, we consider when ladder graphs are prime and when the corresponding labeling may be done in a cyclic manner around the vertices of the ladder. Furthermore, we discuss coprime labelings for complete bipartite graphs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMinimally Unbalanced Diamond-Free Graphs and Dyck-Paths (https://doi.org/10.1137/140981496)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; No characterization of minimally unbalanced graphs is known, and even no conjecture on the structure of such graphs has been posed, contrary to what happened for perfect graphs. ... Theory B, 77 (1999), pp. ... Rao, Structural properties and decomposition of linear balanced matrices, Math. ... Rao, Testing balancedness and perfection of linear matrices, Math. ... Deutsch, Dyck path enumeration, Discrete Math., 204 (1999), pp.\n\n# Minimally Unbalanced Diamond-Free Graphs and Dyck-Paths\n\nAuthors: Nicola Apollonio and Anna GalluccioAuthors Info & Affiliations\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1137/140981496\n\nGet Access\n\nBibTeX\n\nTools\n\n## Abstract\n\nA $\\{0,1\\}$-matrix $\\mathsf{A}$ is balanced if it does not contain a submatrix of odd order having exactly two 1's per row and per column. A graph is balanced if its clique-matrix is balanced. No characterization of minimally unbalanced graphs is known, and even no conjecture on the structure of such graphs has been posed, contrary to what happened for perfect graphs. In this paper, we provide such a ch