njectured that the permutations of every multiset admit an imperfect Hamiltonian traversal by adjacent swaps: a walk in the neighbor-swap graph that visits every word, with some words visited twice in order to reach a neighbor and return. The question is posed as an unsolved research problem in Knuth's Art of Computer Programming. Verhoeff (2017) chose the stutter words, in which every domino is a double, as the words to be reached this way, and reformulated the conjecture as the Hamiltonicity of the graph $N(S)$ on the non-stutter words, with two exceptional families --- binary signatures with an odd multiplicity, and the permutations of $(2k,1,1)$ --- that admit a Hamiltonian path but no cycle. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv Troller (https://arxiv-troller.com/?q=paper%3A+2610.09975)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Using the connection between bent-sum decompositions and derivatives of bent functions, we also obtain an infinite family of balanced Boolean functions that cannot be derivatives of bent functions. ... We prove that for every integer $k\\ge2$ and every rational $r\\in[0,1]$ there exists a $k$-uniform hypergraph $F_{k,r}$ such that \\begin{equation*} \\mathrm{ex}_k(n,F_{k,r})=Θ(n^{k-r}). \\end{equation*} The case $k=2$ is the rational exponents conjecture of Erdős and Simonovits \\cite{Erdos1988}, also known as Erdős problem #571 \\cite{BloomErdoswebsite}; a proof was announced in September 2026 and is expounded by Conlon et al.\\ \\cite{Conlon-Janzer-Jiang-Kim-Lee-Liu-Longbrake-Yepremyan}. ... This paper proves asymptotically optimal linear degree lower bounds for Nullstellensatz and Polynomial Calculus, yielding a four-way polynomial-versus-exponential proof-size classification for uniform polynomial consequences over fields of positive characteristic.\nFor $k\\ge3$ the theorem extends their result to $k$-uniform hypergraphs. In the single-hypergraph setting, this strengthens Fitch's finite-family theorem \\cite{Fitch} by realizing each exponent with a single forbidden $k$-uniform hypergraph for $0\\le r\\le1$. We also show, via an explicit two-edge construction, that for $k\\ge4$ this strengthening fails for $\\lceil k/2\\rceil\n\nDegree profiles of uniform polynomial consequences: Linear lower bounds for Nullstellensatz and Polynomial Calculus\n\nSøren Riis\n\narXiv:2611.07280\n\nAuthors: Søren Riis\n\nSubmitted: Nov 2026 | Updated: Nov 2026\n\nThis paper proves asymptotically optimal linear degree lower bounds for Nullstellensatz and Polynomial Calculus, yielding a four-way polynomial-versus-exponential proof-size classification for uniform polynomial consequences over fields of positive characteristic. The size bounds use full, explicit coefficient lists, including zeros. Apart from a fixed finite initial range, exactly one of four cases occurs: $\\mathrm T_1$, bounded degree; $\\mathrm T_2$, degree at least linear in the universe size $n$; $\\mathrm T_3$, constant degree on some residue classes and at least linear degree on the others; or $\\mathrm T_4$, infinitely many recurring finite degrees, with bounded-degree subsequences and finite $Ω(n)$ peaks at arbitrarily large sizes. Thus the size patterns are polynomial bounds, exponential lower bounds, a periodic mixture, or polynomial subsequences with finite exponential peaks, respectively. Underivable targets have degree $\\infty$, and both proof systems assign the same type. The result strengthens my 2008--2009 refutation classification and extends it to uniform consequences. The proof uses degree-preserving lifting with onset and period bounds linear in the degree. A common $p$-adic hardness spectrum describes the four types topologically. Distinguishing any two types is undecidable already for refutations. The linear degree bounds, four-way classification and pairwise undecidability results have been formalised and verified in Lean. The Lean formalisation, verification records and